# mirror-parable fix log

## epoch 0 (2026-11-25): created from a reference reel
Made by Claude from the rea-video breakdown of references/controller.mp4 (59.9 s, 576 x 1024, code-rendered 2D motion graphics on pure black, 11 shots by the survey, 79% held frames, almost no frame change outside a few moves; TTS narrator at about 3.3 words/s, female-leaning pitch about 223 Hz, wide pitch movement; a soft bed at about 80 bpm; one pop sound effect), see reference-lab/refs/controller (survey + review by Claude) and reference-lab/runs/controller/crops.
Taken from the reference (idea and art, not its objects):
- Idea: a second-person parable built on a double standard. A tiny symbolic scene where a figure tagged FRIEND fails and gets a calm, exact answer from you; the same failure as YOU gets a harsh verdict; both replayed side by side with their words in chips; the same pattern in real life with an expectation curve that turns and lands on the real outcome; the outside view ("if it were your friend's") as the one full-colour reveal; back to the opening scene with the advice applied, so the reel loops.
- Look: pure black frame, one soft elliptical light pool per scene (centre lightness about 0.15, edges 0), the scene in one horizontal band in the middle of the frame, a glowing white disc-and-bell figure with arm stubs and no face, thin bevelled dark slabs with a bright top edge, dashed baselines and measuring ticks, pill chips (dark with a grey border for who is who, white with dark text for spoken words, dimmed grey for the rejected words), a mint expectation curve (measured lightness 0.86, chroma 0.07, hue about 178; glow 0.58) over a tilted grid plate with a counter riding its tip, the crash in red (lightness 0.56 to 0.62, chroma 0.14, hue about 20), outlined numerals, warm low-chroma greys for the real-life room with a warm lamp, and one full-colour reveal (blue-teal field hue about 230 with an ochre spot hue about 75, lightness 0.71, chroma 0.10). Colours measured with tools/oklch_colours.py and a pixel sampler on full-resolution frames.
- Left behind on purpose (user: "taking the idea and art, not the objects"): the topic (self-talk), the game controller and the jump-between-platforms level, the desk mat, laptop, phone and store UIs, all words and numbers. The template's symbolic set is any simple obstacle built from slabs and is never a game device, screen or vehicle; the third asset is whatever real place or object the study is about.
Changes from the reference for clarity: the figure about 270 px tall (the reference's is about 75 px on a 1080 frame), chips 38 to 40 px (reference about 26 px), the expect and reality numbers must be real measured values from the reference (the reference's numbers are imagined story numbers).
Engine limits met on the way (not changed; ask before touching the engine):
- Captions: the reference shows the whole spoken line in bold white sentence case with no box, directly under the scene (about y 1300). The engine has no such mode; the template uses "captions: plain" (whole line, no box, but lowercase, regular weight, at y about 1600), so the scene band sits a little lower (y 640 to 1400).
- The writer's fact checker sees only the script: the parable's imagined scenes ("your friend ...") may be flagged as unsupported claims. If it happens, the factcheck prompt needs a line that second-person hypothetical scenes are not claims.
- The reference has no transition smear; the engine adds one at every shot start.
- Subject size check switched off: the hero (the figure) is small on its set by design.
First brief (chosen by Claude, a different topic from the reference): "The spotlight effect: why you think everyone noticed your embarrassing moment".

## epoch 0 story passes (2026-11-25, --until script, MiniMax $0.035 + $0.018)
- reels/spotlight-s1 ("The spotlight effect: why you think everyone noticed your embarrassing moment"): the Wikipedia article (and its fetched passages) states no study numbers (no expected vs measured share), so the expect beat had no counter and the payoff landed on "drastic overestimation ... is widely common". The fact checker also flagged the imagined parable lines (the friend's question, "same mistake, same result") as unsupported claims and the revision flattened them into explanations. Fixes: the fact sheet must hold one study with BOTH expected and measured numbers (ask "need" for its passages), the payoff is the measured number; the parable beats get the double-standard fact or none; the parable's quoted question, clipped echo and "why" line survive revisions. Brief changed to one whose article states the numbers.
- reels/planning-s1 ("The planning fallacy: why your own plans always take longer than you think"): real numbers (37 students, thesis estimate 33.9 days, actual 55.5 days), fact check clean, viewer 4/4/5/4. Flaws -> fix: the hook opened with the brief's question and an abstract "why did I underestimate" instead of a concrete failure -> line 1 starts "Your friend" and is an everyday failure as an action; the reality line added a derived difference (two numbers in one shot) -> the landing line carries no number; parable shots say no numbers.
Engine note (not changed): the fact checker (engine/prompts/factcheck.txt) gets no template guidance; a parable framed in the second person can be flagged as invented. A line there ("an imagined second-person scene that illustrates the topic is not a claim") would protect any story template.

