## epoch 4 (2026-11-25): redesign after the user's review of the epoch-2 reel (reels/planning-e3-v2)
User: "it looks bad and not so good, you'll have to watch it and make the fixes", and "I said to make the new reel on a completely different brief, you made the brief on the same reel" (spotlight effect and planning fallacy were the reference's own field: self-talk / psychology).
Watched planning-e3 at 2 frames a second and at full resolution against the reference: a chunky toilet-sign figure with T-bar arms about 3 times the reference's size; a hard grey oval behind every scene; a set (slab, box, ramp) that says nothing; a muddy olive room with a yellow capsule lamp; small lowercase grey captions where the reference has bold white ones; 30 s stretches where nothing changes; a vague story.
Redesign (the old version is in the scratchpad as mirror-parable.epoch3.md):
- Subject-free: the reference's idea kept as a device for any field: a small everyday case mirrors the big real thing (the reference's game mirrors real life), the two side by side in identical halves, what you see or guess against what is really there as two numbers, one full-colour reveal of the hidden part, a loop back to the small case. The friend / you self-talk parable is gone; the small case is a true everyday fact, so the fact checker has nothing imagined to flag.
- Background: exact kit.bg code, tested with the engine's own validateKit (passes the flatness and motion checks with soft gradients and specks only), so M3 has no reason to add shapes.
- Figure: exact drawing recipe, about 146 px tall, one arm stub, like the reference's.
- Objects: the reference's laptop line-art (dark fills, pale 2.5 px outlines, a glowing key part), each subject with a hidden part dashed at colour 0 and in the reveal colours at colour 1.
- Numbers: two glowing 3D columns on the perspective plate on one shared scale (works for any pair of numbers), numbers pop whole.
- Captions: "phrases" (bold white chunks, number words in the first hue), closer to the reference's bold white lines than "plain".
- 8 shots: hook, closer, compare, real, first, second, reveal, return.
Brief (a different field: physics / nature): "Icebergs: why about 90% of an iceberg is hidden under the water". Story pass reels/iceberg-s1 ($0.02): ice cube in water -> waterline -> "an iceberg does the same thing" -> one-tenth above -> ninety percent below -> ice less dense than seawater -> the cube again; fact check clean, viewer 4/3/4/4.

